      "id": "g3ygoqo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Background of Fundamental Duties"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Fundamental Duties were not part of the original Constitution in 1950; they were added upon the recommendations of the Swaran Singh Committee.",
        "The 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976 introduced Part IV-A and Article 51A, incorporating 10 Fundamental Duties.",
        "The 11th Fundamental Duty was added by the 86th Constitutional Amendment Act in 2002, focusing on education for children aged 6 to 14 years.",
        "Fundamental Duties are non-justiciable in nature and apply strictly to Indian citizens, not foreigners.",
        "They serve as a constant reminder to citizens that while enjoying rights, they must also perform specific civic and moral obligations towards the nation."
      ],
      "id": "290g5u6"
    },
